\title{Kahn's Algorithm analysis}
\date{2026-10-16T21:46:56Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{How long does [Kahn's Algorithm](00NI) take?

\ul{
  \li{Creating #{T}, #{Z}, and #{\mathit{in}} each take #{\Theta(1)}.}
  \li{Initializing #{\mathit{in}} (the first \sf{for} loop) takes #{\Theta(V+E)} since we loop over all vertices and then look at every edge once.}
  \li{Initializing #{Z} (the second \sf{for} loop) takes #{\Theta(V)}, assuming we can add to #{Z} in #{\Theta(1)} time.}
  \li{The main \sf{while} loop will execute #{\Theta(V)} times; in each loop we do some #{\Theta(1)} operations (remove from #{Z}, append to #{T}), and do #{\Theta(1)} work for each of several edges. Again, we will look at each edge once in total, so this main loop takes #{\Theta(V+E)} time.}
}

Overall, then, this algorithm is #{\Theta(1) + \Theta(V+E) + \Theta(V)
+ \Theta(V+E) = \Theta(V+E)}.}
